---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RecordLookupTool from '../components/RecordLookupTool.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="DNSKEY Record Lookup" description="DNSKEY record lookup: inspect a zone's DNSSEC public keys, flags, and algorithms.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns-records/" class="hover:text-accent">Record Types</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">dnskey Record Lookup</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNSKEY Record Lookup</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-dim">DNSKEY records hold DNSSEC public keys. Verify KSK/ZSK presence and algorithm strength.</p>
    <div class="mt-8"><RecordLookupTool type="DNSKEY" label="DNSKEY Record Lookup" hint="Lookups run in your browser via public DNS-over-HTTPS. Nothing is logged." /></div>
    <article class="rich-text mt-12 max-w-none">
      <h2>How to read the results</h2>
      <p>DNSKEY answers are the zone's public keys: flag 257 marks the key-signing key referenced by the parent's DS, 256 the zone-signing key for everyday records.</p>
      <p>Check the algorithm field: 13 (ECDSA P-256) or 15 (Ed25519) are modern and compact; long RSA keys work but bloat responses.</p>
      <div class="grid gap-2"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How often should I check this record?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">After any change to it, after provider switches, and whenever symptoms point this direction. Records do not drift on their own.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do these lookups work for subdomains?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: enter any hostname. Subdomains carry their own records for many types.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why do results differ from my DNS panel?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Panels show the authoritative zone; this shows what resolvers actually serve, which can be a cached earlier answer within TTL.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is this lookup cached on my side?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The query runs fresh in your browser; any caching is the resolver's own, exactly as the wider internet experiences it.</p></details></div>
      
      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/dnskey-record/">DNSKEY records explained (guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dns-record-lookup/">All-in-one record lookup</a> and <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
